// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`the runbook's self-hosted monitoring > renders the stack on its own server (snapshot) 1`] = `
"## Self-hosted monitoring

Prometheus 3.15.1, Loki 3.7.9 and Grafana 13.3.0 run on its own server \`monitoring\`. Grafana Alloy 1.21.3 on each of
this environment's servers sends them the server's and its containers' figures, Caddy's and Postgres's where they run, and
the app's log lines, masked on the server before they leave it. None of it is reachable from the internet.

- **Open Grafana** with \`opscrew addon open monitoring\`: it gives Grafana's private address when this computer is a device
  on the private network, or else keeps an SSH tunnel open to http://localhost:3000. Log in as \`admin\` with
  \`monitoring.grafanaAdminPassword\` in \`~/.opscrew/projects/shop-0123456789ab/generated_values.json\` on the machine that set the stack up (never in this repository), or in its recovery copy.
- **The dashboards** are in Grafana's folder OpsCrew: servers, containers, HTTP traffic, Caddy, Postgres and logs, each with
  the app, the environment and the server to choose. They are read-only: save a changed one as a copy.
- **Figures are kept 30 days** (at most 24 GB) **and logs 7 days.** \`opscrew addon status\` shows the stack's
  containers, the disk they take, and when each server's last figures and log line arrived; a server silent for more than
  5 minutes is flagged with what to check.
- **Alerts** stay with the scheduled check: the stack sends none.
- **Turn it off** with \`opscrew addon disable monitoring\`, then \`opscrew provision\`, which removes what every server runs for monitoring, then deletes server \`monitoring\`, with its figures and logs, only after you type its name. It is refused while other environments send to it.

"
`;

exports[`the runbook's self-hosted monitoring > renders the stack on the app's server (snapshot) 1`] = `
"## Self-hosted monitoring

Prometheus 3.15.1, Loki 3.7.9 and Grafana 13.3.0 run on the app's server. Grafana Alloy 1.21.3 on each of
this environment's servers sends them the server's and its containers' figures, Caddy's and Postgres's where they run, and
the app's log lines, masked on the server before they leave it. None of it is reachable from the internet.

- **Open Grafana** with \`opscrew addon open monitoring\`: it gives Grafana's private address when this computer is a device
  on the private network, or else keeps an SSH tunnel open to http://localhost:3000. Log in as \`admin\` with
  \`monitoring.grafanaAdminPassword\` in \`~/.opscrew/projects/shop-0123456789ab/generated_values.json\` on the machine that set the stack up (never in this repository), or in its recovery copy.
- **The dashboards** are in Grafana's folder OpsCrew: servers, containers, HTTP traffic, Caddy, Postgres and logs, each with
  the app, the environment and the server to choose. They are read-only: save a changed one as a copy.
- **Figures are kept 30 days** (at most 24 GB) **and logs 7 days.** \`opscrew addon status\` shows the stack's
  containers, the disk they take, and when each server's last figures and log line arrived; a server silent for more than
  5 minutes is flagged with what to check.
- **Alerts** stay with the scheduled check: the stack sends none.
- **Turn it off** with \`opscrew addon disable monitoring\`, then \`opscrew provision\`, which removes what every server runs for monitoring, and the stack; the stack's figures and logs are deleted only after you type the confirmation it asks for.

"
`;

exports[`the runbook's self-hosted monitoring > renders the stack on the repository's monitoring server (snapshot) 1`] = `
"## Self-hosted monitoring

Prometheus 3.15.1, Loki 3.7.9 and Grafana 13.3.0 run on the repository's monitoring server \`monitoring\`, which the environment of \`opscrew/plan.yaml\` owns. Grafana Alloy 1.21.3 on each of
this environment's servers sends them the server's and its containers' figures, Caddy's and Postgres's where they run, and
the app's log lines, masked on the server before they leave it. None of it is reachable from the internet.

- **Open Grafana** with \`opscrew addon open monitoring\`: it gives Grafana's private address when this computer is a device
  on the private network, or else keeps an SSH tunnel open to http://localhost:3000. Log in as \`admin\` with
  \`monitoring.grafanaAdminPassword\` in the generated values of the environment of \`opscrew/plan.yaml\`, on the machine that set the stack up (never in this repository), or in its recovery copy.
- **The dashboards** are in Grafana's folder OpsCrew: servers, containers, HTTP traffic, Caddy, Postgres and logs, each with
  the app, the environment and the server to choose. They are read-only: save a changed one as a copy.
- **Figures are kept 30 days** (at most 24 GB) **and logs 7 days.** \`opscrew addon status\` shows the stack's
  containers, the disk they take, and when each server's last figures and log line arrived; a server silent for more than
  5 minutes is flagged with what to check.
- **Alerts** stay with the scheduled check: the stack sends none.
- **Turn it off** with \`opscrew addon disable monitoring\`, then \`opscrew provision\`, which removes this environment's agents and takes the monitoring server off this environment's network; the server and the other environments' data stay.

"
`;
